// Practice quiz: try/catch/finally and try-with-resources
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/exceptions-and-logging/try-catch-finally-and-try-with-resources

::o1 catch order::FileNotFoundException extends IOException. In which order should their catch blocks appear after one try? {
=catch (FileNotFoundException e) first, then catch (IOException e)#Correct. The more specific type goes first, so each block can be reached.
~catch (IOException e) first, then catch (FileNotFoundException e)#Not quite. This does not compile, because IOException already catches every FileNotFoundException.
~Any order, because Java picks the best match#Not quite. Java tries catch blocks from top to bottom, so order matters.
~Only one catch block is allowed after a try#Not quite. A try can have several catch blocks.
}

::o1 why specific::Why catch NumberFormatException around Integer.parseInt rather than catching Exception? {
=Only the failure you expect and can handle is caught, so other bugs still show up#Correct. A broad catch also swallows problems you did not plan for.
~catch (Exception e) does not catch NumberFormatException#Not quite. It does, because NumberFormatException is a subclass of Exception.
~A specific catch makes the program faster#Not quite. The reason is correctness, not speed.
~The compiler requires the exact type#Not quite. The compiler accepts a broader type here.
}

::o2 finally with return::A method body is try \{ return "A"; \} finally \{ System.out.println("F"); \}. What happens when it is called? {
=It prints F, then the method returns "A"#Correct. finally runs before control leaves the try, even on a return.
~It returns "A" without printing F#Not quite. A return does not skip finally.
~It prints F and returns null#Not quite. The value "A" is still returned.
~It does not compile#Not quite. return inside try with a finally is allowed.
}

::o2 finally skipped::In which case does a finally block not run? {
=System.exit is called inside the try#Correct. System.exit stops the JVM, so the finally never runs.
~The try block throws an exception#Not quite. finally runs whether or not an exception is thrown.
~The catch block throws a new exception#Not quite. finally still runs before the new exception leaves.
~The try block returns a value#Not quite. finally runs before the method returns.
}

::o3 when closed::A reader is opened in a try-with-resources header. When is it closed? {
=Automatically when the try block ends, normally or because of an exception#Correct. try-with-resources calls close for you on every way out of the block.
~Only when the block ends without an exception#Not quite. It is closed on the exception path too.
~Only if you call close in a finally block#Not quite. That manual step is exactly what try-with-resources replaces.
~When the garbage collector removes it#Not quite. Closing is immediate and predictable, not left to the garbage collector.
}

::o3 what qualifies::What can be declared in a try-with-resources header? {
=An object whose class implements AutoCloseable#Correct. try-with-resources needs a close method it can call, which AutoCloseable guarantees.
~Any object at all#Not quite. Only AutoCloseable objects, which include every Closeable, are allowed.
~Only files#Not quite. Streams, readers, database connections and other AutoCloseable objects work too.
~Only objects created with new on the same line#Not quite. An effectively final variable declared earlier can also be used.
}
